{"paper_id":"2509.23292","title":"Learning How to Use Tools, Not Just When: Pattern-Aware Tool-Integrated Reasoning","abstract_text":"Tool-integrated reasoning (TIR) has become a key approach for improving large reasoning models (LRMs) on complex problems. Prior work has mainly studied when to invoke tools, while overlooking how tools are applied. We identify two common patterns: a calculator-pattern that uses code for direct computation, and an algorithmic-pattern that encodes problems as programs. Misaligned choices often cause failures even when reasoning is sound. We propose a two-stage framework that first builds code competence from both patterns and then aligns pattern selection with teacher preferences. Across challenging math datasets, our pattern-aware method substantially improves both code usage and accuracy-for instance, raising Code@1 on MATH500 from 64.0% to 70.5% and on AIME24 from 26.7% to 50.0%. These gains highlight the effectiveness of a pattern-aware approach for tool-integrated reasoning.","body_text":"IntroductionTool-integrated reasoning (TIR) has become a powerful paradigm for enhancing large reasoning models (LRMs) [57,8,77]. By interacting with external verifiers such as code interpreters, TIR enables models to produce executable reasoning steps, making answers more accurate, faithful, and verifiable [84]. Recent progress has shown gains from better timing of tool invocation, multi-round tool calls, and tighter integration of external computation [19,57].While effective, a critical question remains: can models reliably decide how to use tools once invoked? We provide an initial exploration of this problem. Models often misalign their tool-use strategies with problem demands, applying code in a mechanical and context-insensitive way that prevents the tool from working effectively. As Figure 1 illustrates, on the factorial problem 1000! ÷ (800! × 2!) the model used the calculator-pattern and failed due to overflow, while on the \"first 10-digit prime in π\" problem it used the algorithmic-pattern and stalled without output. In both cases, switching to the complementary pattern succeeds, indicating that the failures stem from pattern selection rather than a lack of reasoning ability.To address this challenge, we propose a two-stage learning framework. In the first stage, code competence acquisition, the model learns to generate reliable code from both calculatorand algorithmic-pattern data, without teacher preferences, ensuring stable tool use.In the second stage, pattern preference alignment, teacher signals guide the model to choose the Calculate Calculator: Use Python as a code Algorithmic: Use Python as a tool To Calculate the , I will use python factorial function from math library to calculate , and and then divide them to get the final result import math nu = math.factorial(1000) d1 = math.factorial(800) d2 = math.factorial(100) result = nu / (d1 * d2) print(result) Find the first 10-digit prime number in the decimal expansion of To calculate the first 10-digit prime number that appears in the decimal expansion of PI, I will use python to get the every 10 digit and then check if its prime or not. import mpmath mpmath.mp.dps = 11 pi_str = str(mpmath.mp.pi) [2:] print(pi_str) Okay so the first 10 digits are 1415926535, now I will use python to find out if its prime or not from sympy import isprime print(isprime(1415926535)) # False Okay so the first 10 digits are not prime, lets get the next 10 digits. import mpmath .... .... Okay so the first 1000 digi&lt;/EOS&gt; To calculate the first 10-digit prime number that appears in the decimal expansion of PI, I will use python to run a simulation which will check every 10 digits from the PI and see if its prime or not using library. import mpmath from sympy import isprime mpmath.mp.dps = 1000000 pi = mpmath.mp.pi pi_str = str(pi)[2:] def find_prime_in_pi(): ... find_prime_in_pi() # Found prime: 5926535897 Algorithmic: Use Python as a tool Calculator: Use Python as a code ✅ 2609335896245315522158.... ❌ OverflowError: integer division result too large for a float ✅ 5926535897 ❌ LimitError: Context Limit Reached The calculation of can be simplified by first expanding the factorial in the numerator. By canceling the term from the numerator and the denominator, the expression simplifies to: Given that , the final calculation is: Now, I will use python to calculate the product. result = 500 for i in range(801, 1000): result = result * i an algebraic approach succeeds while direct computation overflows. Right: in finding the first 10-digit prime in π, a symbolic approach fails from context limits while a scanning approach succeeds. These cases show that success depends on the chosen tool-use pattern.more suitable pattern for each problem. Together, these stages equip LRMs to not only invoke tools effectively but also adapt their strategy of use.Our experiments on challenging math benchmarks show substantial and consistent gains. The model's ability to produce executable code (@1) increases from 64.0% to 70.5% on MATH500, indicating that code competence acquisition markedly stabilizes tool use. At the same time, overall problem-solving accuracy rises from 26.7% to 50.0% on AIME24, demonstrating that pattern preference alignment further improves strategy selection and reduces failures due to mismatched tool use. Together, these results validate that modeling how tools are used-beyond merely invoking them-yields large improvements in both code reliability and end-task performance.This work makes three contributions. (1) We identify and formalize pattern mismatch in TIR, showing that misaligned tool-use strategies can derail otherwise sound reasoning. (2) We propose a two-stage framework that first builds code competence from both calculatorand algorithmic-pattern data (without teacher preferences) and then aligns pattern choice with teacher signals.(3) We provide empirical evidence on MATH500 and AIME24 that this approach substantially improves code@1 (64.0% → 70.5%) and accuracy (26.7% → 50.0%), and we illustrate how switching patterns flips failure cases into successes (Fig. 1).\nRelated Works\nReasoning with LRMsAmong various LLM abilities, foundational research in semantic matching [68,66] and temporal QA [67] has paved the way for complex understanding. While domain adaptation [26] and test-time integration [25] enhance robustness, reasoning ability achieved by post-training [24,34,32] stands as a key frontier. Given that traditional SFT may suffer from incomplete learning [65], recent works introduce uncertainty-aware reward modeling [64], dense feedback from LM critics [3], and preference-driven code generation [39] to refine alignment. Moreover, recent studies have advanced role-playing reasoning through humanactor emulation [5] and improved continual learning via prototype-conditioned generative replay [6].The scope of reasoning has expanded into multimodal domains, where models now \"think with 3D\" [12] and utilize reinforced visual perception [13,11]. Advanced frameworks like OmniVideo-R1 [14] and DV-Matcher [10] further integrate audio-visual and geometric cues. Such multimodal intelligence significantly boosts retrieval performance, including chat-driven search [61,60] and vision-driven video representation [63,62]. Furthermore, safeguarding techniques [76,33] and hallucination mitigation [75,28] ensure the reliability of these complex systems.To support deployment, system efficiency and continual learning have become indispensable.Inference is optimized via multi-tier KV cache management [16], selective sharing [17], and adaptive MoE scheduling [15,53]. Moreover, lightweight techniques like parameter-efficient sampling [74], structured pruning [41], and distillation [38] enable efficient task execution, from medical imaging [37] to web agents [18]. Finally, reversible alignment [58] and unified LoRA generation [59,9] allow models to learn continually, while comprehensive interaction surveys [40] and robust federated learning [7] continue to push the boundaries of scalable AI.Among LLMs' various abilities [69,83,45], subsequent work has focused on improving the quality of these reasoning paths, either by introducing diversity through self-consistency [56], refining intermediate steps via process supervision [42,31], or encouraging verification of intermediate results [79,54,82].Beyond core architecture, recent research emphasizes a data-centric perspective on the entire LLM lifecycle [47], where mathematical reasoning is further enhanced by adversarial data synthesis [78] and iterative DPO with dynamic sampling [48]. To improve model robustness and learning efficiency, advanced techniques such as difficulty-based sample weighting [85], implicit data augmentation [87], and adversarial training with anti-adversaries [86] have been introduced.In the context of embodied AI and spatio-temporal reasoning, unified token spaces for human-object interaction [73] and object-centric decoupling for robotic manipulation [71] have improved explainable decision-making. These abilities are further integrated into 3D scene understanding [72] and autonomous driving through spatio-temporal Chain-of-Thought (CoT) [80] and dual-memory vision-language navigation [81]. Furthermore, the application of multi-scale graph learning and Transformers has shown significant potential in predicting complex real-world dynamics, including financial fraud detection [35], traffic flow prediction [52], disaster risk assessment [50], mental health [70], recommendations [55] and socio-economic metrics such as labor market gradients [43] and business resilience indices [51].\nTool-Integrated ReasoningTool-integrated reasoning has emerged as a powerful paradigm for enhancing large reasoning models (LRMs) [57,8], enabling executable reasoning steps through external tools such as Python interpreters [84,30]. Early work like PoT [4], PAL [20], and MathPrompter [27] showed that converting reasoning into code execution or lightweight snippets can substantially improve math performance, while implementations such as ToRA [22] and Qwen2.5-Math-Instruct-TIR [49] further demonstrated gains from deeper integration. However, prior studies primarily focus on when to call tools, overlooking how tools are applied once invoked. We address this gap by modeling pattern selection in TIR, introducing a pattern-aware framework that aligns tool-use strategies and yields significant improvements in reasoning accuracy.\nMethod\nAlgorithmic-pattern\nAlgorithmic SolutionCalculator Solution\nProblem ProblemCode Competence Acquisition Problem 2: A Problem 2: C ….\nN Data\nStudent\n2N DataProblem 1: A Problem 1: CWe train a pattern-aware reasoning model in two stages. For stage 1, we builds code competence by supervising the model on both calculator-style and algorithmic-style solu-tions. Stage 2 performs pattern preference alignment, using Direct preference optimization (DPO) to learn the pattern preference.\nTraining set constructionWe distinguish two usage patterns in toolintegrated reasoning: the calculator-pattern, where code is used only for direct computation or verification, and the algorithmicpattern, where problems are expressed as full programs. For each problem, we generate a Chosen Solution with the more suitable pattern and a Counter Solution with the alternative one. This dual construction not only equips the model with competence in both styles of code usage, but also provides the supervision necessary to align its pattern selection with teacher preferences, thereby improving robustness and accuracy in downstream reasoning tasks. And We put the complete version is provided in Section A.\nCode Competence AcquisitionFor each problem x, we construct two solutions:• an algorithmic-style solution y alg that converts the problem to an execuable program;• a calculator-style solution y calc that keeps the reasoing path while using python for arithmetic or verification.We write both solutions with the same ouput shcema (reasoning → python block → ouputs → final answer), which can simplifies parsing and execution. Let D SF T = {(x i , y alg i ), (x i , y calc i )} be the set of both patterns, we minimize the negative log-likelihood over response tokens:L SFT = -E (x,y)∼D SFT |y| t=1 log π θ (y t | x, y &lt;t ),\nPattern Preference AlignmentWe use DPO [46] to empower our model with the ability to pick the correct pattern to solve math problems. For each problem x, we collect two candidate completions y alg , y calc and label a winner y + and loser y -using the teacher model. For a pair (x, y + , y -), we then apply direction preference optimization with a frozen reference policy π ref (our SFT model):L DPO = -log σ β log π θ (y + | x) -log π θ (y -| x) -log π ref (y + | x) -log π ref (y -| x) ,where β scales preference strength and σ is the logistic function. This increases the likelihood of preferred (pattern-appropriate) solutions relative to dispreferred ones, without training a separate reward model.\nExperiments\nDatasetWe use google Gemini-2.5-flash-lite as our teacher model, using our designed two pattern prompt to solve the problems in OpenR1-Math-220k [44] dataset. From this corpus, we select the first 10,000 problems and divide them into training and validation sets using a 9:1 ratio. To assess complex mathematical reasoning and generalization, we evaluate on a broad set of out-of-domain benchmarks, including MATH500 [23], AIME24 [1], and AMC23 [2].\nEvaluation MetricsWe report Pass@1 as the primary accuracy metric. For additional insight into reasoning behavior, we also track (i) the proportion of problems where the model's output contains executable code (Code@1), and (ii) the proportion of problems where such code is both present and leads to the correct final answer (Code+Pass@1). According to Table 1, the base model achieves near-zero code usage and accuracy. With SFT, the model begins to leverage Python extensively (e.g., R1Math Code@1 rises to 66%), and correctness follows accordingly. Adding DPO yields further substantial improvements, notably pushing GSM8K Code@1 from 72.0% to 80.1% and AIME24 from 26.7% to 50.0%, while also boosting correctness (e.g., AIME24 Code+Pass@1 from 6.6% to 26.7%). These results highlight that SFT is crucial for enabling tool use, whereas DPO markedly enhances both adoption and accuracy.\nMain Results\nMethod\nConclusionIn this work, we studied two complementary tool-use patterns-algorithmic code generation and calculator-style usage-and showed that many failures arise from mismatched pattern choices rather than reasoning ability. Experiments on GSM8K, MATH500, and AIME24 demonstrate that our pattern-aware approach improves strategy selection and accuracy, offering an effective and lightweight way to enhance tool-integrated reasoning.\nA Complete Prompt\nDouble Pattern PromptUse a python interpreter as a tool to solve the following math problem. There are two possible usage patterns:• Pattern A: Treat the problem as a coding problem, write a complete solution using Python code.• Pattern B: Treat the Python interpreter as a simple calculator, only using it for arithmetic or verification when needed. Your task:1. Decide which pattern (A or B) is more appropriate for this problem. Call this the Chosen Solution. 2. Provide the Chosen Solution with EXACTLY ONE continuous reasoning paragraph (no lists, no numbering, no bullet points). Then give ONE Python code block, then the code outputs, then the final answer. 3. Provide the Counterfactual Solution using the other pattern with the SAME constraints (one continuous reasoning paragraph, one code block, outputs, final answer). 4. Strict constraints: -Do NOT restate the problem text. -Do NOT include introductions, apologies, or meta comments. -Do NOT duplicate any content across fields. Output format (pure JSON, no backticks, no extra keys):{ \" problem \": \" &lt; the math problem here &gt;\" , \" chosen_pattern \": \" A or B \" , \" chosen_solution \": { \" reasoning \": \" &lt; one continuous paragraph &gt;\" , \" code_blocks \": [\" &lt; one python block only &gt;\"] , \" outputs \": [\" &lt; stdout lines &gt;\"] , \" final_answer \": \"...\" } , \" counter_solution \": { \" reasoning \": \" &lt; one continuous paragraph &gt;\" , \" code_blocks \": [\" &lt; one python block only &gt;\"] , \" outputs \": [\" &lt; stdout lines &gt;\"] , \" final_answer \": \"...\" } }\nB Additional Related Works on LLMS Reasoning CapacityAmong various LLM abilities, foundational research in semantic matching [68,66] and temporal QA [67] has paved the way for complex understanding. While domain adaptation [26] and test-time integration [25] enhance robustness, reasoning ability achieved by post-training [24,34] stands as a key frontier. Given that traditional SFT may suffer from incomplete learning [65], recent works introduce uncertainty-aware reward modeling [64], dense feedback from LM critics [3], and preference-driven code generation [39] to refine alignment. Moreover, recent studies have advanced role-playing reasoning through humanactor emulation [5] and improved continual learning via prototype-conditioned generative replay [6].Figure 1 :1Figure1: Pattern mismatch in tool-integrated reasoning. Left: in 1000! ÷ (800! × 2!), an algebraic approach succeeds while direct computation overflows. Right: in finding the first 10-digit prime in π, a symbolic approach fails from context limits while a scanning approach succeeds. These cases show that success depends on the chosen tool-use pattern.\nTable 1 :1Pass@1 and Code+Pass@1 accuracy on R1-Distill-Qwen models across R1Math, GSM8K, MATH500, and AIME24.R1MathGSM8K (OOD)MATH500 (OOD)AIME24 (OOD)Code@1 Code+Pass@1 Code@1 Code+Pass@1 Code@1 Code+Pass@1 Code@1 Code+Pass@1R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5BBase3.0%2.0%4.2%4.2%8.0%6.0%0.0%0.0%+SFT66.0%55.5%72.0%70.4%64.0%58.2%26.7%6.6%+SFT+DPO72.2%70.3%80.1%77.4%70.5%63.2%50.0%26.7%","sections":[{"title":"Introduction","text":"Tool-integrated reasoning (TIR) has become a powerful paradigm for enhancing large reasoning models (LRMs) [57,8,77]. By interacting with external verifiers such as code interpreters, TIR enables models to produce executable reasoning steps, making answers more accurate, faithful, and verifiable [84]. Recent progress has shown gains from better timing of tool invocation, multi-round tool calls, and tighter integration of external computation [19,57].While effective, a critical question remains: can models reliably decide how to use tools once invoked? We provide an initial exploration of this problem. Models often misalign their tool-use strategies with problem demands, applying code in a mechanical and context-insensitive way that prevents the tool from working effectively. As Figure 1 illustrates, on the factorial problem 1000! ÷ (800! × 2!) the model used the calculator-pattern and failed due to overflow, while on the \"first 10-digit prime in π\" problem it used the algorithmic-pattern and stalled without output. In both cases, switching to the complementary pattern succeeds, indicating that the failures stem from pattern selection rather than a lack of reasoning ability.To address this challenge, we propose a two-stage learning framework. In the first stage, code competence acquisition, the model learns to generate reliable code from both calculatorand algorithmic-pattern data, without teacher preferences, ensuring stable tool use.In the second stage, pattern preference alignment, teacher signals guide the model to choose the Calculate Calculator: Use Python as a code Algorithmic: Use Python as a tool To Calculate the , I will use python factorial function from math library to calculate , and and then divide them to get the final result import math nu = math.factorial(1000) d1 = math.factorial(800) d2 = math.factorial(100) result = nu / (d1 * d2) print(result) Find the first 10-digit prime number in the decimal expansion of To calculate the first 10-digit prime number that appears in the decimal expansion of PI, I will use python to get the every 10 digit and then check if its prime or not. import mpmath mpmath.mp.dps = 11 pi_str = str(mpmath.mp.pi) [2:] print(pi_str) Okay so the first 10 digits are 1415926535, now I will use python to find out if its prime or not from sympy import isprime print(isprime(1415926535)) # False Okay so the first 10 digits are not prime, lets get the next 10 digits. import mpmath .... .... Okay so the first 1000 digi&lt;/EOS&gt; To calculate the first 10-digit prime number that appears in the decimal expansion of PI, I will use python to run a simulation which will check every 10 digits from the PI and see if its prime or not using library. import mpmath from sympy import isprime mpmath.mp.dps = 1000000 pi = mpmath.mp.pi pi_str = str(pi)[2:] def find_prime_in_pi(): ... find_prime_in_pi() # Found prime: 5926535897 Algorithmic: Use Python as a tool Calculator: Use Python as a code ✅ 2609335896245315522158.... ❌ OverflowError: integer division result too large for a float ✅ 5926535897 ❌ LimitError: Context Limit Reached The calculation of can be simplified by first expanding the factorial in the numerator. By canceling the term from the numerator and the denominator, the expression simplifies to: Given that , the final calculation is: Now, I will use python to calculate the product. result = 500 for i in range(801, 1000): result = result * i an algebraic approach succeeds while direct computation overflows. Right: in finding the first 10-digit prime in π, a symbolic approach fails from context limits while a scanning approach succeeds. These cases show that success depends on the chosen tool-use pattern.more suitable pattern for each problem. Together, these stages equip LRMs to not only invoke tools effectively but also adapt their strategy of use.Our experiments on challenging math benchmarks show substantial and consistent gains. The model's ability to produce executable code (@1) increases from 64.0% to 70.5% on MATH500, indicating that code competence acquisition markedly stabilizes tool use. At the same time, overall problem-solving accuracy rises from 26.7% to 50.0% on AIME24, demonstrating that pattern preference alignment further improves strategy selection and reduces failures due to mismatched tool use. Together, these results validate that modeling how tools are used-beyond merely invoking them-yields large improvements in both code reliability and end-task performance.This work makes three contributions. (1) We identify and formalize pattern mismatch in TIR, showing that misaligned tool-use strategies can derail otherwise sound reasoning. (2) We propose a two-stage framework that first builds code competence from both calculatorand algorithmic-pattern data (without teacher preferences) and then aligns pattern choice with teacher signals.(3) We provide empirical evidence on MATH500 and AIME24 that this approach substantially improves code@1 (64.0% → 70.5%) and accuracy (26.7% → 50.0%), and we illustrate how switching patterns flips failure cases into successes (Fig. 1).","level":1,"path_id":"1","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":0,"section_id":"sec-1"},{"title":"Related Works","text":"","level":2,"path_id":"2","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":1,"section_id":"sec-2"},{"title":"Reasoning with LRMs","text":"Among various LLM abilities, foundational research in semantic matching [68,66] and temporal QA [67] has paved the way for complex understanding. While domain adaptation [26] and test-time integration [25] enhance robustness, reasoning ability achieved by post-training [24,34,32] stands as a key frontier. Given that traditional SFT may suffer from incomplete learning [65], recent works introduce uncertainty-aware reward modeling [64], dense feedback from LM critics [3], and preference-driven code generation [39] to refine alignment. Moreover, recent studies have advanced role-playing reasoning through humanactor emulation [5] and improved continual learning via prototype-conditioned generative replay [6].The scope of reasoning has expanded into multimodal domains, where models now \"think with 3D\" [12] and utilize reinforced visual perception [13,11]. Advanced frameworks like OmniVideo-R1 [14] and DV-Matcher [10] further integrate audio-visual and geometric cues. Such multimodal intelligence significantly boosts retrieval performance, including chat-driven search [61,60] and vision-driven video representation [63,62]. Furthermore, safeguarding techniques [76,33] and hallucination mitigation [75,28] ensure the reliability of these complex systems.To support deployment, system efficiency and continual learning have become indispensable.Inference is optimized via multi-tier KV cache management [16], selective sharing [17], and adaptive MoE scheduling [15,53]. Moreover, lightweight techniques like parameter-efficient sampling [74], structured pruning [41], and distillation [38] enable efficient task execution, from medical imaging [37] to web agents [18]. Finally, reversible alignment [58] and unified LoRA generation [59,9] allow models to learn continually, while comprehensive interaction surveys [40] and robust federated learning [7] continue to push the boundaries of scalable AI.Among LLMs' various abilities [69,83,45], subsequent work has focused on improving the quality of these reasoning paths, either by introducing diversity through self-consistency [56], refining intermediate steps via process supervision [42,31], or encouraging verification of intermediate results [79,54,82].Beyond core architecture, recent research emphasizes a data-centric perspective on the entire LLM lifecycle [47], where mathematical reasoning is further enhanced by adversarial data synthesis [78] and iterative DPO with dynamic sampling [48]. To improve model robustness and learning efficiency, advanced techniques such as difficulty-based sample weighting [85], implicit data augmentation [87], and adversarial training with anti-adversaries [86] have been introduced.In the context of embodied AI and spatio-temporal reasoning, unified token spaces for human-object interaction [73] and object-centric decoupling for robotic manipulation [71] have improved explainable decision-making. These abilities are further integrated into 3D scene understanding [72] and autonomous driving through spatio-temporal Chain-of-Thought (CoT) [80] and dual-memory vision-language navigation [81]. Furthermore, the application of multi-scale graph learning and Transformers has shown significant potential in predicting complex real-world dynamics, including financial fraud detection [35], traffic flow prediction [52], disaster risk assessment [50], mental health [70], recommendations [55] and socio-economic metrics such as labor market gradients [43] and business resilience indices [51].","level":2,"path_id":"2.1","parent_section_id":"sec-2","order_in_parent":0,"section_id":"sec-2.1"},{"title":"Tool-Integrated Reasoning","text":"Tool-integrated reasoning has emerged as a powerful paradigm for enhancing large reasoning models (LRMs) [57,8], enabling executable reasoning steps through external tools such as Python interpreters [84,30]. Early work like PoT [4], PAL [20], and MathPrompter [27] showed that converting reasoning into code execution or lightweight snippets can substantially improve math performance, while implementations such as ToRA [22] and Qwen2.5-Math-Instruct-TIR [49] further demonstrated gains from deeper integration. However, prior studies primarily focus on when to call tools, overlooking how tools are applied once invoked. We address this gap by modeling pattern selection in TIR, introducing a pattern-aware framework that aligns tool-use strategies and yields significant improvements in reasoning accuracy.","level":2,"path_id":"2.2","parent_section_id":"sec-2","order_in_parent":1,"section_id":"sec-2.2"},{"title":"Method","text":"","level":3,"path_id":"3","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":2,"section_id":"sec-3"},{"title":"Algorithmic-pattern","text":"","level":1,"path_id":"1~2","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":3,"section_id":"sec-1~2"},{"title":"Algorithmic Solution","text":"Calculator Solution","level":1,"path_id":"1~3","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":4,"section_id":"sec-1~3"},{"title":"Problem Problem","text":"Code Competence Acquisition Problem 2: A Problem 2: C ….","level":1,"path_id":"1~4","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":5,"section_id":"sec-1~4"},{"title":"N Data","text":"","level":1,"path_id":"1~5","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":6,"section_id":"sec-1~5"},{"title":"Student","text":"","level":1,"path_id":"1~6","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":7,"section_id":"sec-1~6"},{"title":"2N Data","text":"Problem 1: A Problem 1: CWe train a pattern-aware reasoning model in two stages. For stage 1, we builds code competence by supervising the model on both calculator-style and algorithmic-style solu-tions. Stage 2 performs pattern preference alignment, using Direct preference optimization (DPO) to learn the pattern preference.","level":1,"path_id":"1~7","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":8,"section_id":"sec-1~7"},{"title":"Training set construction","text":"We distinguish two usage patterns in toolintegrated reasoning: the calculator-pattern, where code is used only for direct computation or verification, and the algorithmicpattern, where problems are expressed as full programs. For each problem, we generate a Chosen Solution with the more suitable pattern and a Counter Solution with the alternative one. This dual construction not only equips the model with competence in both styles of code usage, but also provides the supervision necessary to align its pattern selection with teacher preferences, thereby improving robustness and accuracy in downstream reasoning tasks. And We put the complete version is provided in Section A.","level":2,"path_id":"3.1","parent_section_id":"sec-3","order_in_parent":0,"section_id":"sec-3.1"},{"title":"Code Competence Acquisition","text":"For each problem x, we construct two solutions:• an algorithmic-style solution y alg that converts the problem to an execuable program;• a calculator-style solution y calc that keeps the reasoing path while using python for arithmetic or verification.We write both solutions with the same ouput shcema (reasoning → python block → ouputs → final answer), which can simplifies parsing and execution. Let D SF T = {(x i , y alg i ), (x i , y calc i )} be the set of both patterns, we minimize the negative log-likelihood over response tokens:L SFT = -E (x,y)∼D SFT |y| t=1 log π θ (y t | x, y &lt;t ),","level":2,"path_id":"3.2","parent_section_id":"sec-3","order_in_parent":1,"section_id":"sec-3.2"},{"title":"Pattern Preference Alignment","text":"We use DPO [46] to empower our model with the ability to pick the correct pattern to solve math problems. For each problem x, we collect two candidate completions y alg , y calc and label a winner y + and loser y -using the teacher model. For a pair (x, y + , y -), we then apply direction preference optimization with a frozen reference policy π ref (our SFT model):L DPO = -log σ β log π θ (y + | x) -log π θ (y -| x) -log π ref (y + | x) -log π ref (y -| x) ,where β scales preference strength and σ is the logistic function. This increases the likelihood of preferred (pattern-appropriate) solutions relative to dispreferred ones, without training a separate reward model.","level":2,"path_id":"3.3","parent_section_id":"sec-3","order_in_parent":2,"section_id":"sec-3.3"},{"title":"Experiments","text":"","level":4,"path_id":"4","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":9,"section_id":"sec-4"},{"title":"Dataset","text":"We use google Gemini-2.5-flash-lite as our teacher model, using our designed two pattern prompt to solve the problems in OpenR1-Math-220k [44] dataset. From this corpus, we select the first 10,000 problems and divide them into training and validation sets using a 9:1 ratio. To assess complex mathematical reasoning and generalization, we evaluate on a broad set of out-of-domain benchmarks, including MATH500 [23], AIME24 [1], and AMC23 [2].","level":2,"path_id":"4.1","parent_section_id":"sec-4","order_in_parent":0,"section_id":"sec-4.1"},{"title":"Evaluation Metrics","text":"We report Pass@1 as the primary accuracy metric. For additional insight into reasoning behavior, we also track (i) the proportion of problems where the model's output contains executable code (Code@1), and (ii) the proportion of problems where such code is both present and leads to the correct final answer (Code+Pass@1). According to Table 1, the base model achieves near-zero code usage and accuracy. With SFT, the model begins to leverage Python extensively (e.g., R1Math Code@1 rises to 66%), and correctness follows accordingly. Adding DPO yields further substantial improvements, notably pushing GSM8K Code@1 from 72.0% to 80.1% and AIME24 from 26.7% to 50.0%, while also boosting correctness (e.g., AIME24 Code+Pass@1 from 6.6% to 26.7%). These results highlight that SFT is crucial for enabling tool use, whereas DPO markedly enhances both adoption and accuracy.","level":2,"path_id":"4.2","parent_section_id":"sec-4","order_in_parent":1,"section_id":"sec-4.2"},{"title":"Main Results","text":"","level":5,"path_id":"5","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":10,"section_id":"sec-5"},{"title":"Method","text":"","level":1,"path_id":"1~8","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":11,"section_id":"sec-1~8"},{"title":"Conclusion","text":"In this work, we studied two complementary tool-use patterns-algorithmic code generation and calculator-style usage-and showed that many failures arise from mismatched pattern choices rather than reasoning ability. Experiments on GSM8K, MATH500, and AIME24 demonstrate that our pattern-aware approach improves strategy selection and accuracy, offering an effective and lightweight way to enhance tool-integrated reasoning.","level":6,"path_id":"6","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":12,"section_id":"sec-6"},{"title":"A Complete Prompt","text":"","level":1,"path_id":"1~9","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":13,"section_id":"sec-1~9"},{"title":"Double Pattern Prompt","text":"Use a python interpreter as a tool to solve the following math problem. There are two possible usage patterns:• Pattern A: Treat the problem as a coding problem, write a complete solution using Python code.• Pattern B: Treat the Python interpreter as a simple calculator, only using it for arithmetic or verification when needed. Your task:1. Decide which pattern (A or B) is more appropriate for this problem. Call this the Chosen Solution. 2. Provide the Chosen Solution with EXACTLY ONE continuous reasoning paragraph (no lists, no numbering, no bullet points). Then give ONE Python code block, then the code outputs, then the final answer. 3. Provide the Counterfactual Solution using the other pattern with the SAME constraints (one continuous reasoning paragraph, one code block, outputs, final answer). 4. Strict constraints: -Do NOT restate the problem text. -Do NOT include introductions, apologies, or meta comments. -Do NOT duplicate any content across fields. Output format (pure JSON, no backticks, no extra keys):{ \" problem \": \" &lt; the math problem here &gt;\" , \" chosen_pattern \": \" A or B \" , \" chosen_solution \": { \" reasoning \": \" &lt; one continuous paragraph &gt;\" , \" code_blocks \": [\" &lt; one python block only &gt;\"] , \" outputs \": [\" &lt; stdout lines &gt;\"] , \" final_answer \": \"...\" } , \" counter_solution \": { \" reasoning \": \" &lt; one continuous paragraph &gt;\" , \" code_blocks \": [\" &lt; one python block only &gt;\"] , \" outputs \": [\" &lt; stdout lines &gt;\"] , \" final_answer \": \"...\" } }","level":1,"path_id":"1~10","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":14,"section_id":"sec-1~10"},{"title":"B Additional Related Works on LLMS Reasoning Capacity","text":"Among various LLM abilities, foundational research in semantic matching [68,66] and temporal QA [67] has paved the way for complex understanding. While domain adaptation [26] and test-time integration [25] enhance robustness, reasoning ability achieved by post-training [24,34] stands as a key frontier. Given that traditional SFT may suffer from incomplete learning [65], recent works introduce uncertainty-aware reward modeling [64], dense feedback from LM critics [3], and preference-driven code generation [39] to refine alignment. Moreover, recent studies have advanced role-playing reasoning through humanactor emulation [5] and improved continual learning via prototype-conditioned generative replay [6].","level":1,"path_id":"1~11","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":15,"section_id":"sec-1~11"},{"title":"Figure 1 :","text":"1Figure1: Pattern mismatch in tool-integrated reasoning. Left: in 1000! ÷ (800! × 2!), an algebraic approach succeeds while direct computation overflows. Right: in finding the first 10-digit prime in π, a symbolic approach fails from context limits while a scanning approach succeeds. These cases show that success depends on the chosen tool-use pattern.","level":1,"path_id":"1~12","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":16,"section_id":"sec-1~12"},{"title":"Table 1 :","text":"1Pass@1 and Code+Pass@1 accuracy on R1-Distill-Qwen models across R1Math, GSM8K, MATH500, and AIME24.R1MathGSM8K (OOD)MATH500 (OOD)AIME24 (OOD)Code@1 Code+Pass@1 Code@1 Code+Pass@1 Code@1 Code+Pass@1 Code@1 Code+Pass@1R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5BBase3.0%2.0%4.2%4.2%8.0%6.0%0.0%0.0%+SFT66.0%55.5%72.0%70.4%64.0%58.2%26.7%6.6%+SFT+DPO72.2%70.3%80.1%77.4%70.5%63.2%50.0%26.7%","level":1,"path_id":"1~13","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":17,"section_id":"sec-1~13"}],"citations":[{"raw_text":"Ai-MoTeam\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\tAIMO Validation Set -AIME Subset\n\t\t\n\t\t\t2024","doi":null,"arxiv_id":null,"title":null,"anchors":[{"ref_id":"b0","section_path_id":"4.1","quote":"[1]","offset":423}]},{"raw_text":"Ai-MoTeam\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\tAIMO Validation Set -AMC Subset\n\t\t\n\t\t\t2024","doi":null,"arxiv_id":null,"title":null,"anchors":[{"ref_id":"b1","section_path_id":"1","quote":"(2)","offset":4667},{"ref_id":"b1","section_path_id":"4.1","quote":"[2]","offset":438}]},{"raw_text":"Enhancing reinforcement learning with dense rewards from language model critic\n\t\t\n\t\t\tMengCao\n\t\t\n\t\n\t\n\t\tEMNLP\n\t\t\n\t\t\t2024","doi":null,"arxiv_id":null,"title":"Enhancing reinforcement learning with dense rewards from language model critic","anchors":[{"ref_id":"b2","section_path_id":"2.1","quote":"[3]","offset":470},{"ref_id":"b2","section_path_id":"1~11","quote":"[3]","offset":467}]},{"raw_text":"Program of thoughts prompting: Disentangling computation from reasoning for numerical reasoning tasks\n\t\t\n\t\t\tWenhuChen\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tXueguangMa\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tXinyiWang\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tWilliamWCohen\n\t\t\n\t\tarXiv:2211.12588\n\t\t\n\t\t\t2022\n\t\t\n\t\n\tarXiv preprint","doi":null,"arxiv_id":"2211.12588","title":null,"anchors":[{"ref_id":"b3","section_path_id":"2.2","quote":"[4]","offset":229}]},{"raw_text":"XiChen\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tWeiXue\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tYikeGuo\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tActormind\n\t\t\n\t\tEmulating human actor reasoning for speech role-playing\n\t\t\n\t\t\t2026","doi":null,"arxiv_id":null,"title":null,"anchors":[{"ref_id":"b4","section_path_id":"2.1","quote":"[5]","offset":630},{"ref_id":"b4","section_path_id":"1~11","quote":"[5]","offset":627}]},{"raw_text":"Prototype conditioned generative replay for continual learning in NLP\n\t\t\n\t\t\tXiChen\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tMinZeng\n\t\t\n\t\n\t\n\t\tProceedings of the 2025 Conference of the Nations of the Americas Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies\n\t\t\n\t\t\tLuisChiruzzo\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tAlanRitter\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tLuWang\n\t\t\n\t\tthe 2025 Conference o